nat44-ei: keep partial L4 checksums partial

The NAT44-EI counterpart of 0037. Linux behind a TAP with checksum offload
hands VPP its own TCP and UDP with VNET_BUFFER_OFFLOAD_F_TCP_CKSUM or
_UDP_CKSUM set and only the uncomplemented pseudo-header sum in the L4
checksum field. EI updated that field as a complete checksum in every place
it rewrites a port or address: in2out (slow path, the two-packet and the
single-packet fast loops), out2in (the same three) and hairpinning. The
address change went in with the wrong sign, the port and MSS changes went
in although the device sums those bytes, and hairpinning cleared the UDP
field, which for a partial sum loses the pseudo-header.

For such packets add only the address change, the other way round, and
leave ports and MSS to the device; MSS clamping still rewrites the option.
A UDP field of zero is updated too, being a sum. Packets without the
offload flags keep the old code, behind one flag test on the buffer header.

Verified in an isolated VPP with two TAPs with checksum offload, in/out and
output-feature NAT, static mappings rewriting ports and MSS clamping: every
packet the outside and inside TAPs sent was paired with its received copy,
and each one received partial left with the translated packet's
pseudo-header sum, each one received complete with a complete checksum.
Without this patch the first SYN already carried a wrong field. The
hairpinning changes are exercised once 0039 makes hairpinning work. Test:
tests/integration/integration_nat44_partial_checksum.py --nat ei.

